{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789934542,"nonce":"z/4Af4XNO53/nzNK0aOkg0g44gQ=","title":"Abstract constructors with bodies are never run; `new A(x)` hands back the argument","message":"`abstract A(Array\u003cInt\u003e) { public inline function new(n:Int) { this = []; for (i in 0...n) this.push(i*2); } }`\nthen `new A(3)`: the TAST-\u003eHIR lowering inlines only a single `this = e` body\nand otherwise value-wraps the first argument, so `this` was the Int 3. Also\n`extern overload public inline function new(...)` pairs (haxe.ds.Vector,\nIssue10955-style) shared one `new` symbol, the last declaration winning.\n\nFix: a constructor with a body lowers as a method whose receiver slot is\n`this` (the body assigns it, an appended return hands it back); `new A(args)`\ncalls it with a zero of the underlying type, choosing the overload by arity\n(`new_\u003cn\u003e` symbols past the first). Bodyless declarations (`@:multiType`)\nkeep the value wrap. Imported abstracts resolve the constructor through the\nshared symbol table.","files":null}]}